# Answer 5 · Women's Health Status and Measures to Improve Women's Status as a Whole (CSS 2023)
@py question 2023-7

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Part one is the current health picture with data and causes; part two widens to women's status "as a whole" — health, education, work, law and voice — with examples.
@ask ① ELUCIDATE the CURRENT HEALTH STATUS of women in Pakistan
@ask ② WHAT MEASURES could improve women's STATUS AS A WHOLE? Use SUITABLE EXAMPLES
(1) Introduction: health mirrors status; improving one requires improving all
(2) Maternal health: MMR 186; skilled attendance about two-thirds; rural and Balochistan worst
(3) Nutrition and anaemia: 42% of women anaemic; 40% of children stunted
(4) Reproductive health: fertility 3.6; contraception 34%; early marriage
(5) Cancers, mental health, violence and disasters
(6) Causes: norms, poverty, weak services, few female staff, low literacy
(7) Health measures: LHWs, midwives, nutrition, family planning, referral, telemedicine
(8) Education measures: girls' secondary schools, stipends, female teachers — the Punjab stipend example
(9) Economic measures: safe transport, childcare, protected home-based work — the Sindh 2018 law
(10) Legal measures: enforcement, 18-year marriage age, inheritance — the 2020 property law
(11) Political and social measures: women in decision-making, engaging men — BISP and LHW examples
(12) Conclusion: health, education, work, law and voice rise together
:::

## Decoding the question
Two parts. **"Elucidate the current health status"** asks for a clear, **up-to-date** picture with data. **"What measures could be taken to improve women's status as a whole"** widens the frame beyond health to **all dimensions** — education, employment, law, politics. **"Support your arguments with suitable examples"** means each measure needs a **named programme, law or case**.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Women's health** is complete physical, mental and social well-being across the life cycle. **Women's status** is their overall position — rights, resources, respect and power — relative to men.

#### ii) The argument
Women's health in Pakistan is **poor**, especially in maternal health and nutrition, because women's **status** is low: health reflects education, income, legal protection and voice. Measures to improve women's status "as a whole" must therefore work **across dimensions**, and Pakistan already has examples of what works.

### 2. Maternal health
Pakistan's **maternal mortality ratio** was ==186== per 100,000 live births (**Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey 2019**). Only about **two-thirds** of births were assisted by a skilled provider (PDHS 2017–18). Deaths are highest in **rural areas** and **Balochistan**, where distances are long and female staff scarce. Most deaths — from haemorrhage, hypertension, infection and obstructed labour — are preventable. The Egyptian doctor **Mahmoud Fathalla** described such deaths as the end of a "road" of neglect that begins in girlhood.

### 3. Nutrition and anaemia
About ==42%== of women of reproductive age are **anaemic**, and about ==40%== of children under five are **stunted** (National Nutrition Survey 2018). Women often eat last and least, and anaemic mothers have weaker babies — an **intergenerational cycle**. Anaemia also raises the risk of death from bleeding in childbirth and reduces women's energy for work and study.

### 4. Reproductive health
The **total fertility rate** was about ==3.6==, and only about ==34%== of married women used contraception (PDHS 2017–18). **Early marriage** — the old federal age for girls is **16** — leads to adolescent pregnancies with higher risks. Pakistan's population, about ==241 million== in the **Census 2023**, is growing at about **2.55%** a year.

### 5. Other burdens

#### i) Cancers
**Breast cancer** is the leading cancer among women and is often detected late; cervical cancer is also common. Pakistan began introducing the **HPV vaccine** for girls in ==2025==.

#### ii) Mental health
Depression and anxiety are common, linked to violence, isolation after marriage, forced marriage and pressure to bear sons; services are scarce and stigma keeps women from seeking help.

#### iii) Violence and disasters
Domestic violence harms women's physical and mental health; the ==2022== floods left about ==650,000== pregnant women in affected areas (UNFPA).

### 6. Causes

#### i) Social norms
**Son preference**, early marriage, restricted **mobility** and women's low **decision-making** power over their own care.

#### ii) Economic barriers
**Poverty** and out-of-pocket costs for transport, medicines and private care, which families spend on sons before daughters.

#### iii) Health-system weakness
Public health spending of about **1% of GDP**, rural facilities without staff or drugs, and a shortage of **female** doctors. Many women medical graduates never practise, while rural women will often see only a woman.

#### iv) Low literacy
Female literacy of about ==53%== (Census 2023) limits knowledge of nutrition, family planning and danger signs.

### 7. Health measures

#### i) Community health workers
Strengthen the **Lady Health Worker Programme** (==1994==), which brings family planning, antenatal care and immunisation to homes and has been linked to better maternal and child health. Filling vacant posts and paying LHWs on time are cheap, high-impact steps.

#### ii) Skilled birth attendance and referral
Deploy **community midwives**; ensure ambulances (Rescue 1122) and **blood banks** for emergencies, so that complications are treated before it is too late.

#### iii) Nutrition and family planning
Iron and folic acid, food fortification, and contraception through LHWs and pharmacies.

#### iv) Telemedicine
Connect women doctors working from home to rural clinics — addressing both the shortage of female doctors and women's reluctance to see male doctors.

### 8. Education measures
Education is the foundation of women's status. Build **girls' middle and secondary schools** within reach, recruit **female teachers**, and pay **stipends** for girls' attendance. *Example:* **Punjab's female school stipend** programme (from 2004) raised girls' secondary enrolment. Girls who stay in school marry later and have healthier children, so education is also a health measure. Enforce **Article 25A** (free, compulsory education to age 16).

### 9. Economic measures
Paid work gives women income and voice. Provide **safe transport** and enforce the **Harassment Act 2010**; build **day-care** facilities; protect **home-based and domestic workers**. *Examples:* the **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act 2018** recognised home-based workers' rights; the federal **Maternity and Paternity Leave Act 2020** extended maternity leave and introduced paternity leave. Female labour-force participation of ==24.4%== (2024–25) shows how much remains; women with their own income are better able to pay for and decide on their own health care.

### 10. Legal measures

#### i) Enforce existing laws
Train police, set up **one-stop centres**, and speed up justice — as the **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021** attempts with special courts.

#### ii) Marriage age
Raise the minimum age to **18** everywhere. *Example:* **Sindh** (==2013==) and **Islamabad** (==2025==) have already done so.

#### iii) Inheritance
Help women claim property. *Example:* the **Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act 2020** lets women complain to an ombudsperson.

### 11. Political and social measures

#### i) Women in decision-making
Appoint **women ministers** — there were none in 2025 — and strengthen women's roles in legislatures, local government and the bureaucracy. Women legislators of the **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==) drove many of the laws of 2010–2016.

#### ii) Social protection
*Example:* the **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==) pays cash directly to women, giving them money of their own and some bargaining power.

#### iii) Engaging men and communities
Involve husbands, mothers-in-law and religious leaders in supporting women's health, schooling and work. Campaigns that explain Islam's support for birth spacing, girls' education and women's inheritance have helped change attitudes in some districts.

::: fig cycle | How improvements in one dimension lift women's status as a whole.
@centre Women's status
Education: later marriage, better health, higher earnings
Health: the strength to learn and work
Employment: income, voice, independence
Law: protection and property
Political voice: policies that serve women
:::

#### iv) In simple words
Improving women's health without improving their status is like treating a fever without treating the infection. A woman who can read, earn, own property and speak in decisions will seek care earlier, space her pregnancies and feed her children better — so measures for women's status as a whole are also the best health measures.

### 12. Conclusion
Women's health in Pakistan — an MMR of 186, anaemia in two-fifths of women, high fertility — mirrors their low overall status. Improving it requires more than clinics: it needs **girls' education, decent work, enforced laws and women's voice in decisions**. Pakistan already has working examples — the LHW Programme, Punjab's girls' stipends, BISP, Sindh's home-based workers law and new marriage-age laws. Scaling them up would raise women's status **as a whole**.

## Why this answer scores
- An **up-to-date** health picture with dated data and causes.
- The second part is answered **across dimensions**, as "status as a whole" requires.
- Every measure has a **suitable example** — a named programme, law or case.
- A **cycle figure** shows how the dimensions reinforce each other.
